{0 Causet}

Causet is a framework for building event-driven systems as explicit,
composable state machines, with automatic bug finding through deterministic
simulation.

You define the operations your system can perform and the rules it must
obey. Causet generates simulated test runs that vary inputs, event delivery,
time and modeled failures. It checks your invariants after every input,
shrinks failing runs and gives you the choices needed to reproduce them.

It addresses a common problem in asynchronous software: the behavior of a
system becomes scattered across callbacks, tasks, timers and shared state.
Understanding a transition means reconstructing what ran, in which order,
and which request a late result belonged to, and testing those
interactions reliably is hard.

Causet gives that behavior one structure. Each component owns a piece of
State and changes it only in its reducer, in answer to an Action. It never
touches the world directly: it asks for work with Effects, which Services
carry out, and hears back through Events. So the same components run
against the real world or a simulated one:

{v
          In production                     In a test

   ┌─────────────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────────┐
   │ network, disk, clock    │     │ simulated network, disk │
   │                         │     │ and a virtual clock     │
   └──────▲───────────┬──────┘     └──────▲───────────┬──────┘
          │ Effects   │ Events            │ Effects   │ Events
   ┌──────┴───────────▼──────┐     ┌──────┴───────────▼──────┐
   │ your components:        │     │ the same components;    │
   │ one input at a time,    │     │ the test decides what   │
   │ in a fixed order        │     │ arrives, and when       │
   └─────────────────────────┘     └─────────────────────────┘
v}

A Store runs the components one input at a time, in a fixed order, with
time from its own clock. So a test can deliver answers in any order it
likes, generate thousands of runs, and repeat a failing one exactly.

The cost is more explicit code: every asynchronous step is a named
message, and every lifecycle is State. It pays off when the hard parts are
ordering, timeouts, retries, cancellation and stale results, as in network
protocols, wallets and synchronization engines.

{1 Where to start}

Read {{:https://tizoc.github.io/causet/}the guide}, shared with the Rust and TypeScript ports. Its overview covers the
problem, the model, and one checkout's journey through the guide's example
shop, message by message, then how a test takes control of it. The pages
after it give the rules: authority, scheduling, addressing, determinism and
testing. Every example in it runs here as a test, from [doc/*.md].

Most examples use {!Causet_guide.Shop}, a small application with a cart, a
payment component and a gateway Service. It is ordinary code: read its
source.

{1 Libraries}

- {!Causet}: components, the Registry and Protocol, the Store and its
  scheduler, contexts, mounts, the Event bus, the clock, the timer Service,
  observers, bug reports and invariants.
- [causet.ppx]: [[@@deriving message]] and [[@@deriving debug]].
- {!Causet_observers}: logging through [logs], and execution statistics.
- {!Causet_projection}: projections of an ordered log into derived facts.
- {!Causet_test}: testing and simulation on the real Store.
- {!Causet_guide}: the guide's shop.

The reporting pipeline is a package of its own, [causet-reporting]: it
knows nothing of the Store, and an application wires it in as a Service.
